e than 2 consecutive spaces, \n    then replace all consecutive spaces with - \n    \n    fix_spaces(\"Example\") == \"Example\"\n    fix_spaces(\"Example 1\") == \"Example_1\"\n    fix_spaces(\" Example 2\") == \"_Example_2\"\n    fix_spaces(\" Example   3\") == \"_Example-3\"\n    \"\"\"\n    # First, apply the rule for consecutive spaces (3 or more)\n    text_after_consecutive_replacement = _replace_consecutive_spaces(text)\n\n    # Then, apply the rule for single spaces to the result\n    final_modified_text = _replace_single_spaces(text_after_consecutive_replacement)\n\n    return final_modified_text", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/141": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/141", "solution": "class Character:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates a single character and provides methods to check its type.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\n        self._value = value\n\n    def is_digit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the character is a digit '0'-'9'.\"\"\"\n        return self._value.isdigit()\n\n    def is_latin_letter(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the character is a Latin alphabet letter 'a'-'z' or 'A'-'Z'.\"\"\"\n        return len(self._value) == 1 and self._value.isalpha()\nclass AllowedExtensions:\n    \"\"\"Manages the predefined list of valid file extensions.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._types = ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']\n\n    def contains(self, extension: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the provided extension string is in the allowed list.\"\"\"\n        return extension in self._types\nclass FileNamePrefix:\n    \"\"\"Represents the prefix part of a file name and validates its specific rules.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\n        self._value = value\n\n    def is_empty(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the prefix string is empty.\"\"\"\n        return len(self._value) == 0\n\n    def starts_with_latin_letter(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the prefix starts with a Latin letter.\"\"\"\n        if self.is_empty():\n            return False\n        first_char = Character(self._value[0])\n        return first_char.is_latin_letter()\nclass FileNameExtension:\n    \"\"\"Represents the extension part of a file name and validates it.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\n        self._value = value\n\n    def is_allowed(self, allowed_extensions: 'AllowedExtensions') -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the extension is valid using an AllowedExtensions instance.\"\"\"\n        return allowed_extensions.contains(self._value)\nclass FileName:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the full file name string and provides methods for structural validations.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, full_string: str):\n        self._full_string = full_string\n\n    def get_prefix(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Extracts the substring before the first dot.\"\"\"\n        dot_index = self._full_string.find('.')\n        return self._full_string[:dot_index]\n\n    def get_extension(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Extracts the substring after the first dot.\"\"\"\n        dot_index = self._full_string.find('.')\n        return self._full_string[dot_index + 1:]\n\n    def count_digits(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Counts the number of digits in the full file name.\"\"\"\n        count = 0\n        for char_str in self._full_string:\n            char_obj = Character(char_str)\n            if char_obj.is_digit():\n                count += 1\n        return count\n\n    def has_exactly_one_dot(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the file name contains exactly one dot.\"\"\"\n        return self._full_string.count('.') == 1\ndef file_name_check(file_name):\n    \"\"\"Create a function which takes a string representing a file's name, and returns\n    'Yes' if the the file's name is valid, and returns 'No' otherwise.\n    A file's name is considered to be valid if and only if all the following conditions \n    are met:\n    - There should not be more than three digits ('0'-'9') in the file's name.\n    - The file's name contains exactly one dot '.'\n    - The substring before the dot should not be empty, and it starts with a letter from \n    the latin alphapet ('a'-'z' and 'A'-'Z').\n    - The substring after the dot should be one of these: ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']\n    Examples:\n    file_name_check(\"example.txt\") # => 'Yes'\n    file_name_check(\"1example.dll\") # => 'No' (the name should start with a latin alphapet letter)\n    \"\"\"\n    file_obj = FileName(file_name)\n\n    # Condition: The file's name contains exactly one dot '.'\n    if not file_obj.has_exactly_one_dot():\n        return 'No'\n\n    prefix_str = file_obj.get_prefix()\n    prefix_obj = FileNamePrefix(prefix_str)\n\n    # Condition: The substring before the dot should not be empty\n    if prefix_obj.is_empty():\n        return 'No'\n\n    # Condition: The substring before the dot ... starts with a letter from the latin alphapet\n    if not prefix_obj.starts_with_latin_letter():\n        return 'No'\n\n    extension_str = file_obj.get_extension()\n    extension_obj = FileNameExtension(extension_str)\n    allowed_extensions_obj = AllowedExtensions()\n\n    # Condition: The substring after the dot should be one of these: ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']\n    if not extension_obj.is_allowed(allowed_extensions_obj):\n        return 'No'\n\n    # Condition: There should not be more than three digits ('0'-'9') in the file's name.\n    if file_obj.count_digits() > 3:\n        return 'No'\n\n    return 'Yes'", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/144": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "class Fraction:\n    def __init__(self, numerator: int, denominator: int):\n        self.numerator = numerator\n        self.denominator = denominator\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def parse(fraction_string: str) -> 'Fraction':\n        \"\"\"Parses a string \"<numerator>/<denominator>\" into a Fraction object.\"\"\"\n        num_str, den_str = fraction_string.split('/')\n        return Fraction(int(num_str), int(den_str))\n\n    def multiply(self, other_fraction: 'Fraction') -> 'Product':\n        \"\"\"Multiplies this fraction by another and returns a Product object.\"\"\"\n        new_numerator = self.numerator * other_fraction.numerator\n        new_denominator = self.denominator * other_fraction.denominator\n        return Product(new_numerator, new_denominator)\nclass Product:\n    def __init__(self, numerator: int, denominator: int):\n        self.numerator = numerator\n        self.denominator = denominator\n\n    def is_whole_number(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the product's numerator is perfectly divisible by its denominator.\"\"\"\n        return self.numerator % self.denominator == 0\ndef simplify(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Determines if the product of two fractions, given as strings, evaluates to a whole number.\"\"\"\n    fraction_x = Fraction.parse(x)\n    fraction_n = Fraction.parse(n)\n    \n    product = fraction_x.multiply(fraction_n)\n    \n    return product.is_whole_number()", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/145": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "def order_by_points(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers based on the sum of their digits (primary) and original index (secondary).\n    \"\"\"\n    from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n    @dataclass\n    class _AnnotatedInteger:\n        \"\"\"A data structure to hold an integer's value, its original index, and its calculated sum of digits.\"\"\"\n        value: int\n        original_index: int\n        sum_of_digits: int\n\n    def _calculate_sum_of_digits(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the sum of digits for a given integer, handling negative numbers by taking absolute values.\"\"\"\n        s = 0\n        num_abs = abs(num)\n        while num_abs > 0:\n            s += num_abs % 10\n            num_abs //= 10\n        return s\n\n    if not nums:\n        return []\n\n    annotated_list = []\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        sum_of_digits = _calculate_sum_of_digits(num)\n        annotated_list.append(_AnnotatedInteger(num, i, sum_of_digits))\n\n    annotated_list.sort(key=lambda x: (x.sum_of_digits, x.original_index))\n\n    return [item.value for item in annotated_list]", "base_status": "fail", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [[[1, 11, -1, -11, -12]]], "plus_fail_tests": [[[-5, -12, 9, 15]]]}], "HumanEval/142": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "def sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"\"\n    This function will take a list of integers. For all entries in the list, the function shall square the integer entry if its index is a \n    multiple of 3 and will cube the integer entry if its index is a multiple of 4 and not a multiple of 3. The function will not \n    change the entries in the list whose indexes are not a multiple of 3 or 4. The function shall then return the sum of all entries. \n    \n    Examples:\n    For lst = [1,2,3] the output should be 6\n    For lst = []  the output should be 0\n    For lst = [-1,-5,2,-1,-5]  the output should be -126\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _square(n):\n        \"\"\"Calculates the square of an integer.\"\"\"\n        return n * n\n\n    def _cube(n):\n        \"\"\"Calculates the cube of an integer.\"\"\"\n        return n * n * n\n\n    def _apply_transformation(value, index):\n        \"\"\"Applies the specific transformation (square, cube, or retain) to an integer based on its index.\"\"\"\n        if index % 3 == 0:\n            return _square(value)\n        elif index % 4 == 0: # This condition implicitly means index % 3 != 0 because of the preceding `if`\n            return _cube(value)\n        else:\n            return value\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    for index, value in enumerate(lst):\n        total_sum += _apply_transformation(value, index)\n    \n    return total_sum", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/143": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/143", "solution": "def words_in_sentence(sentence):\n    \"\"\"\n    You are given a string representing a sentence,\n    the sentence contains some words separated by a space,\n    and you have to return a string that contains the words from the original sentence,\n    whose lengths are prime numbers,\n    the order of the words in the new string should be the same as the original one.\n\n    Example 1:\n        Input: sentence = \"This is a test\"\n        Output: \"is\"\n\n    Example 2:\n        Input: sentence = \"lets go for swimming\"\n        Output: \"go for\"\n\n    Constraints:\n        * 1 <= len(sentence) <= 100\n        * sentence contains only letters\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_prime(number):\n        if number < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(number**0.5) + 1):\n            if number % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def parse_sentence_into_words(sentence_text):\n        return [word for word in sentence_text.split(' ') if word]\n\n    def validate_sentence_length(sentence_text):\n        sentence_length = len(sentence_text)\n        if not (1 <= sentence_length <= 100):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Sentence length must be between 1 and 100 characters, got {sentence_length}.\")\n\n    def validate_sentence_characters(sentence_text):\n        if not all(c.isalpha() or c.isspace() for c in sentence_text):\n            raise ValueError(\"Sentence must contain only alphabetic characters and spaces.\")\n\n    validate_sentence_length(sentence)\n    validate_sentence_characters(sentence)\n\n    words = parse_sentence_into_words(sentence)\n\n    prime_length_words = []\n    for word in words:\n        if is_prime(len(word)):\n            prime_length_words.append(word)\n\n    return ' '.join(prime_length_words)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fai